---
id: org.dxos.code-mode-djs
name: CodeModeDjs
version: 0.1.0
---

`@dxos/code-mode-djs` runs code-mode programs — the snippets a model writes for the `eval` tool of
`@dxos/agent-code-mode` — in [djs](https://github.com/earendil-works/djs), a JavaScript engine
compiled to WebAssembly whose whole VM state is one byte arena. It is a spike: it exists to find out
whether djs can replace the worker and in-process sandboxes for the Effect dialect.

Four properties define it:

- **The same programs run unchanged.** A program written for the Effect dialect of
  `@dxos/agent-code-mode` — `yield* Database.query(...)`, `Obj.update`, `Operation.invoke`, `print`,
  `DOCS` — runs here with the same names in scope and the same output [F-1].
- **A session is a VM, not an evaluation.** One VM lives for a conversation. Top-level variables and
  the live objects they hold survive from one `eval` to the next [F-2].
- **A VM is bytes.** A session starts as a copy of a prebuilt image that already holds `effect`,
  `@dxos/echo` and `@dxos/compute` [F-3], and a session can be written out as bytes and read back [F-4].
- **The boundary is a handful of synchronous calls.** The guest does not run an ECHO client. Its
  `Database` is a thin working set that asks the host's database for ECHO JSON rows and hands back
  changed rows, in the row format the working-set export already uses [F-5].

The corpus the end-to-end tests replay is taken from code-mode transcripts [F-6].

## Dependencies

```mdl
uses org.dxos.echo: The Database, Obj, Ref, Query and Filter API the guest implements and the host executes; the ECHO JSON object format rows travel in.
```

```mdl
uses org.dxos.echo-sqlite: The pattern the guest database follows — a Database over a non-resident store that hydrates JSON rows into live objects, observes them with Entity.subscribe and writes them behind.
```

## Ontology

| Term             | Definition                                                                                                                                         |
| ---------------- | -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------- |
| **Host**         | The process that owns the conversation: its `Database.Service`, `Operation.Service` and printer.                                                   |
| **Guest**        | Code running inside the djs VM: the guest runtime and the model's programs.                                                                        |
| **Guest bundle** | One IIFE script holding `effect`, `@dxos/echo`, `@dxos/compute` and the guest runtime, built ahead of time.                                        |
| **Base image**   | The arena of a VM that has run the guest bundle and been collected; every session starts as a copy of it.                                          |
| **Session**      | One VM bound to one host database for the life of a conversation.                                                                                  |
| **Bridge**       | The host functions a session's guest calls: `types`, `query`, `load`, `write`, `refresh`, `invoke`, `print`, `sleep`.                             |
| **Row**          | One object crossing the bridge: `{ id, json, version }`, `json` in the ECHO JSON object format (`Obj.toJSON`), `version` the host's change marker. |
| **Snapshot**     | A session written out as bytes: a header naming the engine build and guest bundle it needs, then the image.                                        |

## Extensions

| Term       | URI                          |
| ---------- | ---------------------------- |
| `uses`     | `dxos.spec.core/uses`        |
| `feat`     | `org.dxos.mdl.feat@1.0`      |
| `scenario` | `org.dxos.mdl.scenario@1.0`  |
| `decision` | `org.dxos.mdl.decision@1.0`  |
| `question` | `org.dxos.mdl.question@1.0`  |

## Architecture

```
 host process                                         djs VM (one per session, wasm)
 ┌──────────────────────────────┐   sync host calls   ┌──────────────────────────────────┐
 │ DjsSandbox (Sandbox)         │ ─────────────────▶  │ guest runtime (from base image)  │
 │  └ Session ── Bridge ────────┼── query/load ─────▶ │  BridgeDatabase (Database)       │
 │       │        │             │ ◀──── rows ──────── │   working set: live Obj per id   │
 │       │        ▼             │ ◀──── write ─────── │   dirty set → JSON records       │
 │       │   host Database      │                     │  Effect dialect bindings         │
 │       │   Operation.Service  │ ◀──── invoke ────── │  session globals (variables)     │
 │       ▼                      │ ◀──── print ─────── │  model program (Effect.gen body) │
 │   snapshot bytes ◀── image ──┼──────────────────── │                                  │
 └──────────────────────────────┘                     └──────────────────────────────────┘
```

A host call is synchronous from the guest's side: the VM parks at the call until the host answers,
also when the host answers with a promise. So `Database.query(...).run` in the guest is one bridge
call and one hydration, with no message channel, no RPC framing, no Automerge and no replication.

## Features

```mdl
feat F-1: Effect dialect compatibility

  req F-1.1:
    when: a program written for the Effect dialect of @dxos/agent-code-mode is evaluated
    then: it runs as the body of an Effect.gen generator with Effect, Database, Filter, Query, Order,
          Obj, Relation, Ref, Type, DXN, Operation, Tools, print and DOCS in scope, exactly the names
          and the prompt that dialect gives the model

  req F-1.2:
    when: the program queries (Database.query with a Filter or a Query, including Order, limit,
          relation and reference traversals), resolves (Database.resolve by type DXN, object URI or
          legacy echo DXN), loads a Ref, adds, updates with Obj.update, removes, or flushes
    then: the effect on the host database and what the program reads back are the same as under the
          in-process sandbox

  req F-1.3:
    when: the program invokes an operation bound by the conversation's skills, by its DXN through
          Operation.invoke or, for a tool with no operation, through Tools[name]
    then: the operation runs on the host through Operation.Service, objects in its input arrive as the
          host's live objects (and as references where its schema takes a Ref, as the worker sandbox
          makes them), and objects in its output arrive in the guest as live objects of the session's
          working set, with their rows, so no load call follows

  req F-1.9:
    when: the program calls Operation.invoke or a Tools entry
    then: it gets an effect that does nothing until the program runs it, and the call is asynchronous
          on the host, so the program decides the schedule — one after another with yield*, or
          together with Effect.all and a concurrency option

  req F-1.4:
    when: the program prints
    then: the host renders each value with the Effect dialect's own printer, so an object prints as the
          same card (relations, children, referrers and the code that fetches them) and the eval tool
          output is the same text

  req F-1.5:
    when: the program fails — a thrown error, a failed effect, a syntax error, an exhausted budget
    then: the evaluation fails with EvaluationError carrying the message, the printed output before the
          failure is kept, and the session stays usable

  req F-1.6:
    when: an evaluation is given a timeout
    then: the timeout is not observed; the fuel budget bounds the evaluation instead, stopping it
          deterministically and leaving the session usable

  req F-1.7:
    when: a host installs the sandbox
    then: it implements Sandbox.Sandbox of @dxos/agent-code-mode and is paired with a dialect whose
          instructions are the Effect dialect's plus a paragraph saying variables persist, so
          evaluate, makeEvalToolkit and makeCodeModeTurnProducer use it with no change

  req F-1.8:
    when: the space registers a relation type
    then: its relations are not rebuilt in the guest — the JSON-schema round trip loses a relation's
          kind and endpoints — so traversals that end at objects work and a relation itself reaches
          the guest only as data, as in the worker sandbox
```

```mdl
feat F-2: Continuity between evaluations

  req F-2.1:
    when: a program declares a top-level const, let, var, function or class
    then: the name is bound in the session, and every later program in the session reads and may
          reassign it

  req F-2.2:
    when: a later program declares a name an earlier one bound
    then: the new declaration replaces the old binding; nothing fails as a redeclaration

  req F-2.3:
    when: a session variable holds an ECHO object across evaluations and the host changed that
          object in between
    then: the next evaluation sees the host's current fields before its first statement runs

  req F-2.4:
    when: an evaluation fails part way
    then: the bindings it made before the failure are kept

  req F-2.5:
    when: names declared inside nested blocks or functions
    then: they stay local to their scope, as JavaScript defines them
```

```mdl
feat F-3: Fast start

  req F-3.1:
    when: the first session of a process is opened
    then: the engine runs the guest bundle once in a fresh VM, collects it and keeps the arena as the
          base image

  req F-3.2:
    when: a session is opened over a database whose set of registered types the engine has not seen
    then: the engine copies the base image, rebuilds those types inside it, and keeps that arena as the
          image for the set, keyed by a hash of the types' schemas

  req F-3.3:
    when: any later session is opened over the same set of types
    then: its VM is a copy of that image with the bridge bound by name; neither the guest bundle nor the
          types are rebuilt, and opening takes a few milliseconds, not tens

  req F-3.4:
    when: the engine build, the guest bundle or the host's registry changes
    then: the affected image is rebuilt rather than reused
```

```mdl
feat F-4: Snapshots

  req F-4.1:
    when: a session is idle between evaluations
    then: snapshot() returns a Uint8Array: a header naming the format version, the engine's image
          descriptor and the guest bundle's hash, followed by the collected arena

  req F-4.2:
    when: a snapshot is restored against a host database
    then: the restored session has every session variable, its working set and its type registry, and
          the next evaluation behaves as it would have in the session that wrote it

  req F-4.3:
    when: a snapshot is restored by an engine with another build or bundle
    then: restore fails with an error that names what differs; it never loads the bytes

  req F-4.4:
    when: a session is snapshotted
    then: pending writes are flushed to the host first, so the bytes never hold a change the host has not
          taken
```

```mdl
feat F-5: Thin boundary

  req F-5.1:
    when: the guest needs data
    then: it calls the bridge, synchronously: query(ast) with the query AST as JSON returns rows,
          load(uri) returns one row or null, refresh(versions) returns the rows whose host version
          differs; the host executes the AST with Query.fromAst against its own database

  req F-5.2:
    when: the guest hydrates a row
    then: there is one live object per id in the working set, decoded from ECHO JSON with refs resolved
          through the guest database, as the SQLite database hydrates its rows

  req F-5.3:
    when: guest objects are added, changed or removed
    then: they are written to the host as JSON records ({ id, json, deleted }) in one write call, before
          the next query (read-your-writes), before an operation is invoked, on flush and at the end of
          every evaluation; the host applies a record to its own live object field by field, or creates
          the object with the record's id

  req F-5.4:
    when: a host already holds rows for the objects a session will need (a working-set export)
    then: seed(rows) places them in the guest working set without a bridge call, because a row's json
          is the same ECHO JSON a working-set row's objectJson holds

  req F-5.5:
    when: the guest uses a Database method the bridge does not carry (feeds, branches, history, blobs,
          migrations)
    then: the call fails with a message naming the method; nothing silently no-ops

  req F-5.6:
    when: values cross the bridge
    then: they are plain data only — rows, records, query ASTs, references as { "/": uri } envelopes;
          no live object, schema class or function crosses
```

```mdl
feat F-6: End-to-end coverage from transcripts

  req F-6.1:
    when: the test suite runs
    then: it replays a corpus of programs taken from code-mode transcripts (memoized model
          conversations and space transcripts) through both this sandbox and the in-process sandbox
          against equally seeded databases, and asserts the same output and the same database state

  req F-6.2:
    when: a corpus program depends on a name bound by an earlier program of its conversation
    then: the corpus keeps the conversation's order and the test replays it in one session
```

## Acceptance

```mdl
scenario T-1: A program written for the in-process sandbox runs unchanged
  given: a database with tasks and the Score operation bound
  when: the Effect dialect program that resolves Task, queries the open ones, scores each with
        Operation.invoke and marks the highest done is evaluated
  then:
    - the output equals the in-process sandbox's output
    - the host database holds the same change
  tags: [F-1]
```

```mdl
scenario T-2: Variables survive between evaluations
  given: a session
  when: eval 1 runs `const tasks = yield* Database.query(Filter.type(Task)).run` and eval 2 runs
        `Obj.update(tasks[0], (t) => { t.title = 'x' }); yield* print(tasks.length)`
  then:
    - eval 2 prints the count eval 1 read
    - the host's first task is titled x
  tags: [F-2]
```

```mdl
scenario T-3: Opening a session reuses the base image
  given: an engine that has opened one session
  when: twenty more sessions are opened
  then:
    - the guest bundle ran exactly once
    - the mean time to open a session is under 10 ms
  tags: [F-3]
```

```mdl
scenario T-4: A snapshot restores into a new engine
  given: a session holding a variable with an ECHO object and one with plain data
  when: it is snapshotted, a new engine is created, and the bytes are restored against the same database
  then:
    - both variables read back in the next evaluation
    - the object variable is live: Obj.update on it reaches the host
  tags: [F-4]
```

```mdl
scenario T-5: A query is one bridge call
  given: a session and a database of 100 tasks
  when: a program queries the open tasks and prints their count
  then:
    - the bridge receives exactly one query call and no load calls
    - no ECHO client, Automerge document or RPC channel is created
  tags: [F-5]
```

```mdl
scenario T-7: Seeded rows answer without a bridge call
  given: a session seeded with the rows of three tasks
  when: a program resolves one of them by its URI
  then:
    - it prints the task's title
    - the bridge receives no query and no load call
  tags: [F-5.4]
```

```mdl
scenario T-6: A runaway loop is stopped and the session survives
  given: a session with a bound variable
  when: a program runs `while (true) {}`
  then:
    - the evaluation fails with an exhausted budget
    - the next evaluation still reads the variable
  tags: [F-1.5, F-2.4]
```

## Changelog

```mdl
decision D-1: Spike djs as a code-mode engine in a new package, specified before it is built.
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1, F-2, F-3, F-4, F-5, F-6]
```

```mdl
decision D-2: The engine supports the full Effect dialect — database queries, operation invocations, types — with feature parity with the existing Effect dialect invoker.
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1]
```

```mdl
decision D-3: Variables are preserved between executions.
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-2]
```

```mdl
decision D-4: Startup is fast: a VM is memoized with the SDK dependencies (effect, echo and the rest) preloaded.
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-3]
```

```mdl
decision D-5: VM state can be serialized to binary and deserialized.
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-4]
```

```mdl
decision D-6: Overhead is low: instead of the client-services boundary, a thin boundary uses the VM's synchronous callbacks to delegate to the host's existing database rather than build a full database client, possibly on top of the existing working-set serialization format.
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-5]
```

```mdl
decision D-7: The end-to-end tests cover everything, using code-mode snippets extracted from DXOS space transcripts.
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-6]
```

```mdl
decision D-8: Session variables are top-level declarations rewritten into assignments to VM globals before the program runs. Fills in [D-3].
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-2.1, F-2.2, F-2.4]
  why: djs keeps globalThis between runs but a program body's declarations are locals of its function; rewriting only the top level keeps nested scoping intact, lets later programs redeclare a name, and keeps bindings made before a failure, which a capture-on-return scheme loses.
  rejected:
    - Returning the locals at the end of the program, which loses them on an early return or a throw.
    - A with-scope over a proxy, which strict mode and djs do not allow.
```

```mdl
decision D-9: The base image is the arena of a VM that ran the guest bundle, copied per session through a public image API added to djs by a pnpm patch. Fills in [D-4].
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-3]
  why: djs already starts every VM as a copy of its own base snapshot, so a copy of a VM that loaded the bundle costs about a millisecond against tens for loading it; the package exposes the arena but not a constructor from it, and a patch keeps the access typed and in one reviewed place until upstream offers it.
  rejected:
    - Reading the sandbox's private fields, which hides an unstable dependency in casts.
    - Running the bundle per session, which costs about 60 ms each.
```

```mdl
decision D-10: A snapshot is the VM image with a header, not a djs continuation dump. Fills in [D-5].
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-4]
  why: Continuation dumps refuse weak collections and private fields, which Effect and ECHO hold; the image captures everything and is already binary. Its cost is that it loads only into the same engine build and bundle, which the header makes an explicit check.
  rejected:
    - Continuation dumps, which cannot write the guest runtime's state.
    - Exporting only the session variables as data, which loses closures and live objects.
```

```mdl
decision D-11: The bridge carries ECHO JSON rows and records, executes query ASTs on the host with Query.fromAst, and keeps the guest working set coherent with a version per row. Fills in [D-6].
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-5]
  why: ECHO JSON is the format a working-set row's objectJson and the SQLite database's bodies already use, so the guest hydrates exactly as the SQLite database does and a host can seed a session from a working-set export; Automerge bytes would need Automerge in the guest, which is what a full client costs.
  rejected:
    - Shipping WorkingSetRow Automerge bytes, which needs Automerge wasm inside the VM.
    - Proxies whose every property read calls the host, which makes Obj.update and printing chatty and breaks identity.
```

```mdl
decision D-12: Printing is rendered on the host by the Effect dialect's own printer, from the values the guest encodes. Fills in [D-2].
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.4]
  why: The card needs relation, child and referrer counts that only the host database can answer cheaply, and one printer keeps the output identical across sandboxes.
```

```mdl
question Q-1: Must a snapshot load into a later djs build, or is same-build enough for the first version?
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-4]
  options:
    - Same build: snapshots are a cache for idle sessions and are dropped on deploy; the conversation's history rebuilds what matters.
    - Portable: add a data-level export of session variables (plain data and object references) that loads into any build.
```

```mdl
question Q-2: Does an operation call block the guest, or may a program run operations concurrently with Effect.all?
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.3]
  options:
    - Block: operations are synchronous host calls, simplest to reason about and to snapshot.
    - Concurrent: operations are async host functions, so Effect.all with concurrency overlaps them.
```

```mdl
decision D-13: A second image is kept per set of registered types, built from the base image the first time the set is seen. Fills in [D-4].
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-3.2, F-3.3, F-3.4, T-3]
  why: Copying the base image costs 3 ms, but rebuilding a workspace's types from JSON schema inside the VM cost about 300 ms per session; keyed by a hash of the schemas, it is paid once per set of types, and opening a session measured 6 ms.
  rejected:
    - Rebuilding the types per session, which made a session slower to open than the base image was to build.
    - Rebuilding each type when the guest first needs it, which moves the cost into the first query of every session.
```

```mdl
decision D-14: The fuel budget bounds an evaluation, and its timeout is not observed. Fills in [D-2].
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.6, T-6]
  why: djs stops a run that exhausts its fuel between two instructions and the VM stays usable, which keeps the session's variables; a wall-clock bound could only dispose the VM, which loses them.
```

```mdl
decision D-15: An object passed where an operation's schema takes a reference is made a reference, as the worker sandbox does; the in-process sandbox rejects it. Fills in [D-2].
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.3]
  why: The worker sandbox is the out-of-process sandbox this replaces, and passing the object is the call models get wrong first.
```

```mdl
decision D-16: The corpus keeps snippets from space transcripts only after replacing every space and object id and dropping snippets with mail content, links, credentials or long string literals; a difference that is the engine's own (an error's wording, a module namespace's key order) is listed by snippet with its reason. Fills in [D-7].
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-6]
  why: The transcripts are of people's spaces and the corpus is committed to a public repository; the listed differences come from V8 against djs, not from the bridge, and listing them keeps every other snippet compared exactly.
```

```mdl
decision D-17: Same build.
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  answers: Q-1
  affects: [F-4]
```

```mdl
decision D-18: Operation calls return effects, and the script controls their execution.
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: user:dmaretskyi
  answers: Q-2
  affects: [F-1.3, F-1.9]
```

```mdl
decision D-19: Operation and tool calls are asynchronous host functions, flushed before they start, so effects a program runs together overlap on the host. Fills in [D-18].
  date: 2026-11-08
  by: agent:claude-code
  for: user:dmaretskyi
  affects: [F-1.9]
  why: A synchronous host call parks the whole VM, so Effect.all could not overlap two operations whatever concurrency it asked for; as async calls the VM keeps running the program's fibers while the host works.
```

